{
  "notes": [
    "Expected orders and family assignments are hand-derived from the specification. Symbols follow creation/id order; Uncategorized is the seeded lowest id.",
    "The client's jQuery parameter serialization omits empty select_category_ids and reject_category_ids arrays, so local searches ignore them and request specs omit those parameters. The server treats an explicitly empty select_category_ids list as select nothing.",
    "branch is reply-only; reply is read-only; group is restricted to the signed-in user group. hidden and secret are invisible.",
    "topics browsing agrees with site.sortedCategories on position-ordered serialized input, except orphan: the new tree treats it as a root while existing eager browsing drops it.",
    "hollow > hidden > orphan also pins that an eligible grandchild reached through an invisible middle category cannot make hollow a family.",
    "A positive parent_category_id combined with include_subcategories:false returns nothing; parent_category_id:-1 and include_subcategories:false both keep top-level categories. Grouping still applies for a nonblank term.",
    "forge is created before blue, and both names start with Blue; only blue starts with the entire term Blue ember. This distinguishes whole-term prefix ranking from first-word prefix ranking.",
    "An explicit groupFamilies:false is sent as group_families:false and bypasses the lazy request cache because the request carries that field; it does not enable grouping.",
    "Non-breaking-space-only and em-space-only terms are present in Discourse and remain one literal search word because stripping and word splitting use only ASCII whitespace. They bypass tree_order and enable requested grouping. Only nbsp contains a non-breaking space, so that term returns one row; no category name contains an em space, so that grouped term returns no rows and empty family_keys. A non-breaking space between words also stays literal.",
    "Fullwidth percent and underscore normalization is server-only coverage in the request spec: PostgreSQL unaccent folds them to ASCII percent and underscore, which must still match literally. The client's folding does not cover these fullwidth forms; this is an accepted difference."
  ],
